OSKit INET TCP — sequence numbers, segment encoders, SACK board
================================================================

Same-module sibling of `tcp.lsysl`. Three groups of utility-grade
code that the rest of the TCP stack reuses on every segment:

* `tcp_seq_{lt,le}` — wrapping 32-bit sequence-number compares.
* `tcp_{read,write}_u32` — big-endian seq/ack field encoders.
* TCP options parsing (`tcp_opts_has_sack_permitted`,
  `tcp_opts_get_wscale`, `tcp_opts_get_ts`, `tcp_opts_apply_sack`)
  and the SACK scoreboard helpers
  (`tcp_sack_board_insert/purge/clear`, `tcp_sack_next_hole`).

Lifted out of `tcp.lsysl` because none of these touch the state
machine — they're pure transforms over the bytes on the wire and
the per-socket scoreboard. Keeping them in their own file makes
the state-machine file easier to follow.

    module oskit.servers


32-bit sequence-number comparisons, wrapping modulo 2^32.

    tcp_seq_lt(a: u32, b: u32) -> int
        val d = i32(a - b)
        if d < 0
            return 1
        0

    tcp_seq_le(a: u32, b: u32) -> int
        val d = i32(a - b)
        if d <= 0
            return 1
        0


TCP seq/ack field helpers (big-endian u32).

    tcp_write_u32(frame: *byte, off: int, v: u32)
        frame[off]     = byte((v >> 24) & u32(0xff))
        frame[off + 1] = byte((v >> 16) & u32(0xff))
        frame[off + 2] = byte((v >>  8) & u32(0xff))
        frame[off + 3] = byte( v        & u32(0xff))

    tcp_read_u32(frame: *byte, off: int) -> u32
        val b0 = u32(frame[off]     & 0xff)
        val b1 = u32(frame[off + 1] & 0xff)
        val b2 = u32(frame[off + 2] & 0xff)
        val b3 = u32(frame[off + 3] & 0xff)
        (b0 << 24) | (b1 << 16) | (b2 << 8) | b3


Walk TCP options in the segment starting at `tcp_off + 20` and
ending at `tcp_off + hdr_len`. Returns 1 if a SACK-permitted
option (RFC 2018 §2, kind=4 len=2) is present, 0 otherwise.

`kind=0` (EOL) terminates. `kind=1` (NOP) is a single-byte
skip. Everything else has `len` at `off+1`; we bounds-check
to tolerate malformed segments without reading past the
header end. Unknown options are skipped.

    tcp_opts_has_sack_permitted(frame: *byte, tcp_off: int,
                                hdr_len: int) -> int
        var off = tcp_off + 20
        val opts_end = tcp_off + hdr_len
        while off < opts_end
            val kind = int(frame[off]) & 0xff
            if kind == TCP_OPT_EOL
                return 0
            if kind == TCP_OPT_NOP
                off += 1
            else
                if off + 1 >= opts_end
                    return 0
                val olen = int(frame[off + 1]) & 0xff
                if olen < 2
                    return 0
                if off + olen > opts_end
                    return 0
                if kind == TCP_OPT_SACK_PERMITTED
                    if olen == 2
                        return 1
                off += olen
        0


RFC 7323 §2 Window Scale option parser. Returns 1 (with `shift`
filled in, clamped to 0..14 per §2.3) if a WS option is present
in the TCP header, 0 otherwise. Only meaningful on SYN segments
since the option is exchanged exactly once during the handshake.

    tcp_opts_get_wscale(frame: *byte, tcp_off: int, hdr_len: int,
                        out shift: int) -> int
        shift = 0
        var off = tcp_off + 20
        val opts_end = tcp_off + hdr_len
        while off < opts_end
            val kind = int(frame[off]) & 0xff
            if kind == TCP_OPT_EOL
                return 0
            if kind == TCP_OPT_NOP
                off += 1
            else
                if off + 1 >= opts_end
                    return 0
                val olen = int(frame[off + 1]) & 0xff
                if olen < 2
                    return 0
                if off + olen > opts_end
                    return 0
                if kind == TCP_OPT_WSCALE
                    if olen == 3
                        var s = int(frame[off + 2]) & 0xff
                        if s > 14
                            s = 14
                        shift = s
                        return 1
                off += olen
        0


RFC 7323 §3 Timestamps option parser. Returns 1 (with `tsval`
and `tsecr` filled in) if a TS option is present, 0 otherwise.
Caller is responsible for the PAWS check and for echoing
`tsval` back as `tsecr` on the next outbound segment via
`ts_recent`.

    tcp_opts_get_ts(frame: *byte, tcp_off: int, hdr_len: int,
                    out tsval: u32, out tsecr: u32) -> int
        tsval = u32(0)
        tsecr = u32(0)
        var off = tcp_off + 20
        val opts_end = tcp_off + hdr_len
        while off < opts_end
            val kind = int(frame[off]) & 0xff
            if kind == TCP_OPT_EOL
                return 0
            if kind == TCP_OPT_NOP
                off += 1
            else
                if off + 1 >= opts_end
                    return 0
                val olen = int(frame[off + 1]) & 0xff
                if olen < 2
                    return 0
                if off + olen > opts_end
                    return 0
                if kind == TCP_OPT_TS
                    if olen == 10
                        tsval = tcp_read_u32(frame, off + 2)
                        tsecr = tcp_read_u32(frame, off + 6)
                        return 1
                off += olen
        0


Merge a new SACK block `[seq, end)` into socket `s`'s scoreboard.
Coalesces with overlapping or adjacent existing blocks; drops
blocks wholly to the left of `snd_una` (cumulative ACK already
covers them). Caller guarantees `seq < end`.

    tcp_sack_board_insert(s: int, left: u32, right: u32)
        if tcp_seq_le(right, tcp_sockets[s].snd_una) != 0
            return
        var nleft = left
        var nright = right
        if tcp_seq_lt(nleft, tcp_sockets[s].snd_una) != 0
            nleft = tcp_sockets[s].snd_una
        var bi = 0
        while bi < TCP_SACK_BOARD
            if tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].in_use != 0
                val bleft = tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].left
                val bright = tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].right
                val overlap = tcp_seq_le(nleft, bright) != 0
                if overlap
                    if tcp_seq_le(bleft, nright) != 0
                        if tcp_seq_lt(bleft, nleft) != 0
                            nleft = bleft
                        if tcp_seq_lt(nright, bright) != 0
                            nright = bright
                        tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].in_use = 0
            bi += 1
        bi = 0
        while bi < TCP_SACK_BOARD
            if tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].in_use == 0
                tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].in_use = 1
                tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].left = nleft
                tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].right = nright
                return
            bi += 1


Purge scoreboard entries covered by the advanced `snd_una`.
Blocks strictly to the left get dropped; blocks straddling the
boundary get left-clipped.

    tcp_sack_board_purge(s: int)
        var bi = 0
        while bi < TCP_SACK_BOARD
            if tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].in_use != 0
                val bright = tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].right
                if tcp_seq_le(bright, tcp_sockets[s].snd_una) != 0
                    tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].in_use = 0
                else
                    val bleft = tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].left
                    if tcp_seq_lt(bleft, tcp_sockets[s].snd_una) != 0
                        tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].left = tcp_sockets[s].snd_una
            bi += 1


Clear all scoreboard entries. Called on full recovery exit and
socket reuse — we don't want a stale block to influence the
next connection or retransmit round.

    tcp_sack_board_clear(s: int)
        var bi = 0
        while bi < TCP_SACK_BOARD
            tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].in_use = 0
            bi += 1
        tcp_sockets[s].sack_rexmit_valid = 0


Parse every `Kind=5` SACK option in the TCP options area and
insert each block into the socket's scoreboard. Unknown,
malformed, or duplicate options are tolerated: the parser
bails early on the first length inconsistency rather than
reading past the header.

    tcp_opts_apply_sack(s: int, frame: *byte, tcp_off: int,
                        hdr_len: int)
        var off = tcp_off + 20
        val opts_end = tcp_off + hdr_len
        while off < opts_end
            val kind = int(frame[off]) & 0xff
            if kind == TCP_OPT_EOL
                return
            if kind == TCP_OPT_NOP
                off += 1
            else
                if off + 1 >= opts_end
                    return
                val olen = int(frame[off + 1]) & 0xff
                if olen < 2
                    return
                if off + olen > opts_end
                    return
                if kind == TCP_OPT_SACK
                    // Block payload: (olen - 2) bytes = 8n,
                    // n ∈ {1..4}. Anything else is malformed.
                    val payload_len = olen - 2
                    if payload_len >= 8
                        if (payload_len % 8) == 0
                            var bo = off + 2
                            val bend_off = off + olen
                            while bo + 8 <= bend_off
                                val blk_left = tcp_read_u32(frame, bo)
                                val blk_right = tcp_read_u32(frame, bo + 4)
                                if tcp_seq_lt(blk_left, blk_right) != 0
                                    tcp_sack_board_insert(s, blk_left, blk_right)
                                bo += 8
                off += olen


Find the next un-SACKed byte `≥ cursor` within `[snd_una, snd_nxt)`,
or `snd_nxt` if no hole remains. Straightforward scan over the
scoreboard: if any block covers `cursor`, jump `cursor` to its
end and restart. Terminates because each jump advances cursor
by ≥ 1 byte and the scoreboard has at most `TCP_SACK_BOARD`
blocks — so at most `TCP_SACK_BOARD + 1` passes.

    tcp_sack_next_hole(s: int, cursor: u32) -> u32
        var c = cursor
        if tcp_seq_lt(c, tcp_sockets[s].snd_una) != 0
            c = tcp_sockets[s].snd_una
        var passes = 0
        while passes <= TCP_SACK_BOARD
            var moved = 0
            var bi = 0
            while bi < TCP_SACK_BOARD
                if tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].in_use != 0
                    val bleft = tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].left
                    val bright = tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].right
                    if tcp_seq_le(bleft, c) != 0
                        if tcp_seq_lt(c, bright) != 0
                            c = bright
                            moved = 1
                bi += 1
            if moved == 0
                return c
            passes += 1
        c
